How it was found, and what it does not fix.

The full macOS suite had never run in CI — the job only ever ran filtered
steps. The first run of `cargo test --all` sat on one line for an hour. libtest
prints `test NAME ... ` before running and terminates the line with the result,
and CI stdout is not a terminal, so the partial line stays in the buffer: the
last name shown is the last test that FINISHED, and the one that hangs is the
next in alphabetical order. That pointed at test_multiple_independent_sessions,
and `sample` on the local process confirmed it exactly:

  tests.rs:90               end of test_multiple_independent_sessions
    drop_glue PtySession
      PtySession::drop      pty_session_unix.rs:139
        PtySession::shutdown pty_session_unix.rs:125
          portable_pty Child::wait
            std::process::Child::wait
              __wait4       <- eight minutes and counting

The three shells told the rest: one in state ?Es — trying to exit, never
getting there — and two untouched, because the drops run in series and the
first never returned. Killing the test binary, which closes the master, made
the stuck shell vanish within the second.

The cycle: the kernel holds a shell inside its own exit while the pty master
stays open and undrained; the master is a field of PtySession, released only
once the drop completes — that is, after the wait. Each waited on the other.

Beaver itself is not affected. OwnedSession runs a beaver-pty-reader thread
that drains the master continuously, including through close(): the loop only
leaves on Ok(0) or Err, which is what closing the master produces. The trap
needs a master held open and never read, which only tests do.

That is also why test_pty_kill changed. It kept its reader alive and unread
across kill(), a state the application never reaches; the other tests passed
only because Drop discards the error. It now drops the reader first, which is
the order OwnedSession::close produces.

What this does not fix: PtySession::spawn hands the caller a reader cloned
from the master, so two owners hold the same descriptor and neither can
guarantee it closes. The bound makes that survivable rather than resolved. The
Windows path has the same unbounded wait, with console.close() likewise after
it rather than before.

Local: 12/12 in 12.9s where the suite used to hang; whole Rust suite 3666
passed, 2 failed in 101s — the two known failures, untouched by this.
